Pop a string that dc's S command pushes on a register.
zdc_program_assignStr() serves both s and S. For s it pops the
register's top and the value, and then replaces the register name on
the stack with the string as the result of the assignment, which the
following instruction pops. For S there is no register name on the
stack, so the same replacement put the string back in place of
itself, and it stayed on the main stack as well as on the register:
"[abc]Sa z p" printed 1. A number given to S was popped as it should
be. For S, just pop the string.

---
 miscutils/bc.c |    6 +++++-
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/miscutils/bc.c
+++ b/miscutils/bc.c
@@ -5886,9 +5886,13 @@
 			RETURN_STATUS(bc_error_stack_has_too_few_elements());
 		bc_vec_pop(v);
 		bc_vec_pop(&G.prog.results);
+		// the string replaces the variable as the assignment's result
+		bc_result_pop_and_push(&res);
+	} else {
+		// "Sr": the string moves from the stack to the register
+		bc_vec_pop(&G.prog.results);
 	}
 
-	bc_result_pop_and_push(&res);
 	bc_vec_push(v, &n2);
 
 	RETURN_STATUS(BC_STATUS_SUCCESS);
